    "language_probability": 1,
    "text": "How many people do you need in a room before two of them probably share a birthday? Gut instinct says around one hundred eighty. The real answer is just twenty three. Here's the trick, you're not comparing birthdays with yours, you're comparing every pair. Twenty three people make two hundred fifty three pairs, each one a small chance at a match. Multiply the odds that every pair misses, and it drops below one half. By seventy people, a match is a ninety nine point nine percent certainty.",
